    "message": "feat(analytics): record what every model on a route did, and why\n\nThe request log holds one row per request, so it could only ever name the\nmodel that answered. `route_attempt` is the index that won and `error_kind`\nis the *final* outcome, which means a request rescued by a fallback was\nrecorded as `status=success` with no trace of what it was rescued from.\n\nMeasured against 21 days of the author's live traffic (150,846 requests):\n1,144 fallbacks succeeded, and for the largest single cohort -- 319 requests\nthat fell back off commandcode/deepseek-v4-pro -- the reason was recoverable\nfrom the database in exactly 0 of them.\n\nThis adds a `request_attempts` side table, one row per model the chain\nreached *or deliberately did not reach*:\n\n    attempt  outcome    model_ref                     kind      duration\n    0        failed     groq/definitely-not-a-real     auth      125 ms\n    1        failed     cerebras/also-not-real         auth      214 ms\n    2        failed     groq/third-not-real            auth       48 ms\n\n`skipped` is the row that pays for itself. A three-model chain that only\never ran one was indistinguishable from a one-model route, so \"the fallback\ndid not help\" and \"the fallback was never asked\" could not be told apart.\nBoth reasons a model goes unreached are now named: benched by recent\nfailures, or the request budget was spent before its turn.\n\nAttempt failures are named by their `FailureKind` where they carry one, so\nthe attempt log has one vocabulary. The request row's own `error_kind` still\nmixes kinds with Python class names; that is left alone here.\n\nDesign notes:\n\n- A side table, not more columns: the number of attempts is a property of the\n  chain, not of the schema, and \"attempt 2 never ran because the budget was\n  gone\" is a fact about an attempt that has no row on the request.\n- Verdicts are collected in memory and published once when the chain settles.\n  An attempt verdict is never worth a database round trip while a client is\n  still waiting for tokens.\n- A new table, so `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` is sufficient -- it is columns\n  on an existing table that need an ALTER, and there are none here.\n- Retention reaches it: attempts are deleted with the request they belong to,\n  and by `clear` .\n\nVerification\n------------\nEnd to end against a real server and real upstreams (throwaway HOME, three\ndeliberately-invalid models), read back through the admin API: three attempts,\neach with its own provider, model, duration, error kind and message. Before\nthis change that row carried `error_kind=authentication, route_attempt=2` and\nnothing else. Rendered in the request detail as a ROUTE ATTEMPTS panel and\nscreenshotted.\n\nGuards mutation-tested, each failing by name:\n\n    M1 a mid-stream failure recorded as success  -> stream_that_dies_after_preflight\n    M2 benched model indistinguishable           -> model_benched_by_recent_failures\n    M3 only the winning attempt published        -> all five attempt tests\n    M4 ExecutionFailure named by class not kind  -> execution_failure_is_named_by_its_kind\n    M5 attempts never written to the database    -> rescued_request_keeps_the_reason\n    M6 retention leaves orphaned attempts        -> attempts_are_deleted_with_the_request\n    M7 attempt timing dropped                    -> stream_that_dies_after_preflight\n\nM1 and M7 initially SURVIVED and the tests were fixed rather than the finding\ndropped: the rescued-request test failed at preflight, which is a different\ncode path from a stream that dies after preflight, and the timing assertion\nwas `(x or 0) >= 0`, which is true of None.\n\nLocal CI: ruff format --check, ruff check, ty clean; pytest 4,804 passed with\nthe documented 6 test_install_ps1_* baseline failures.\n\nNot done\n--------\n`skipped/ejected` will read zero until the health registry outlives a single\nrequest. `MessagesHandler` -- and with it the executor and its\nRouteHealthRegistry -- is constructed per request in routes.py, so the\n3-consecutive-failure threshold resets every time and nothing is ever\nbenched. Confirmed live: four consecutive failures of the same model produced\nzero \"MODEL CHAIN: skipping\" lines. The recording is correct and starts\nworking the moment the lifetime is fixed; that fix belongs with the deadline\nwork, not here.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
